Program1: Write a C program to take input for an array of size $n$, read its elements, and calculate their sum

SourceCode:
#include <stdio.h>

void main(){
    int n,i,s=0;
    printf("Enter the size of array");
    scanf("%d",&n);
    int num[n];
    printf("\n Enter %d elements",n);
    for(i=0;i<n;i++)
    {
        scanf("%d",&num[i]);
    }
    for(i=0;i<n;i++)
    {
        s=s+num[i];
    }
    printf("\n sum=%d",s);
}

Process:
Step 1: Declare variables 'n' (array size), 'i' (loop index), 
        and initialize 's' to 0 (to accumulate the total sum)[cite: 13].

Step 2: Prompt the user to enter the size of the array and read 'n'[cite: 13]:
        scanf("%d", &n);[cite: 13]

Step 3: Declare a Variable Length Array (VLA) of size 'n':
        int num[n];[cite: 13]

Step 4: Use a for loop to read 'n' elements from the user into the array[cite: 13]:
        for(i = 0; i < n; i++)[cite: 13]
        {
            scanf("%d", &num[i]);[cite: 13]
        }
        → Elements are stored consecutively at indices 0 to n-1[cite: 13].

Step 5: Use a second for loop to compute the sum of all elements[cite: 13]:
        for(i = 0; i < n; i++)[cite: 13]
        {
            s = s + num[i];[cite: 13]
        }
        → In each iteration, the current element num[i] is added to 's'[cite: 13].

Step 6: Example trace for n = 5 with elements {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}[cite: 13]:
        - Initial: s = 0[cite: 13]
        - i = 0: s = 0 + 10 = 10[cite: 13]
        - i = 1: s = 10 + 20 = 30[cite: 13]
        - i = 2: s = 30 + 30 = 60[cite: 13]
        - i = 3: s = 60 + 40 = 100[cite: 13]
        - i = 4: s = 100 + 50 = 150[cite: 13]

Step 7: Print the final sum:
        printf("\n sum=%d", s);[cite: 13]

